Yes. “Contact points” is a good way to think about it because behavior does not have to be centrally commanded. You can distribute sensing, feedback, incentives, and affordances across an environment and let behavior emerge from repeated local interactions.

In biology, you see this constantly. Animals learn through classical conditioning, operant conditioning, imitation, social learning, habituation, imprinting, and simple trial-and-error. But there are also more distributed forms: ants lay pheromone trails, termites shape environments that then guide later behavior, birds copy songs, predators and prey alter one another’s behavior over generations, and domesticated animals learn human cues that did not exist in their ancestral environment.

So a useful abstraction is:

contact point → local signal → response → consequence → memory/update → repeated behavior

Then scale it up:

many contact points → patterned environment → coordinated behavior without a single controller

That is where things like stigmergy become interesting: one creature changes the environment, and that environmental change becomes a signal for the next creature. The “message” can live in the world rather than in anyone’s head.

And behavior can move in several directions at once. It can be:

taught by another organism,
learned through consequences,
induced by environmental structure,
selected over generations,
or co-created between organism and environment.

That last one is important. Life does not merely adapt to an environment; it also changes the environment, which changes what gets learned or selected next. Beavers build dams, humans build cities, microbes alter chemistry, plants change soils. The environment becomes part of the memory and control loop.

So for your larger systems view, distributed contact points could serve different functions:

sensor points — detect state.
actuator points — change state.
teaching points — expose consequences or demonstrations.
reward points — reinforce behavior.
boundary points — limit or redirect behavior.
translation points — convert one kind of signal into another.
memory points — leave traces that later agents can read.

That gives you a much more flexible architecture than one central “brain.”

And yes, it is fascinating that life can discover behaviors that were never explicitly designed by any one participant. Sometimes the behavior is trained. Sometimes inherited. Sometimes socially transmitted. Sometimes the environment itself does most of the teaching.

That suggests another clean pattern:

distributed interfaces can become a curriculum.

The world teaches through what it lets organisms sense, what it rewards, what it punishes, and what traces previous organisms leave behind.